# ============================================================
# flow/mst_shared_leg_suffix.buda
#
# The MIRROR of flow/mst_shared_leg_prefix.buda: two MST edges
# meeting at one block where the shared legs END rather than
# START.  STILL OPEN on this branch — the trim matches on shared
# STARTS, so it does not fire here.
#
# `compute_mst` emits every edge with `u < v` and
# `realize_mst_edge` routes `u -> v`, so whether a shared node's
# legs start or end at it is decided purely by that node's index.
#
# THE LEVER IS WHICH BLOCK IS THE DRIVER, not which is declared
# first.  `nodes` for `add_mst_candidates` comes from the bundle's
# BUSTERM list, and the driver heads it — so re-ordering
# `add_block` cannot move the hub's index (measured: hub-first and
# hub-last give byte-identical candidates).  Making the geometric
# hub a RECEIVER does move it: the hub then holds the higher index
# of both its edges, and both legs land on it as segment ENDS.
#
# This is the prefix fixture's geometry with ONE line changed —
# `hub` demoted from driver to receiver, `drv` taking the far
# position the old `far` block held.
#
# Measured on this branch (trim in, and it does not fire):
#
#   MST_VH  seg2 (1890,1010)->(2010,1010) len  120
#             inside seg4 (690,1010)->(2010,1010) len 1320
#   MST_HV  seg4 (2010, 940)->(2010,1010) len   70
#             inside seg2 (2010, 390)->(2010,1010) len  620
#
# both sharing the END point (2010,1010) — the hub's SW corner
# after the corner margin, the same corner the prefix fixture
# shares.
#
# THE HARM IS DIFFERENT IN KIND from the prefix case, which is
# why this is filed separately rather than as "the same bug".
# Here both legs terminate on the hub's face and both get busterm
# taps, so there is no free end and `check_design` reports
# Success.  What is wrong instead:
#
#   * the short leg is PURE DUPLICATE — its long sibling already
#     reaches the same junction, so its 120 units x 4 bits are
#     480 of the vehicle's 11114 detailed WL (~4.3%); and
#   * the perpendicular partner claims an ENDPOINT conn to BOTH
#     legs, which cannot both hold once NUTS puts them on
#     different tracks.  NUTS says so:
#       [NUTS] junction infeasible: bundle 1 seg 1 cannot reach seg 2
#     and those feed `ripup_reroute`'s contender list, so the
#     shape costs healer effort as well as metal.
#
# So the mirror is a WIRELENGTH-and-robustness fix, not a
# dangling-metal one, and it should not be sold as the same
# defect.  The geometric cut is the mirror image: trim the long
# leg's END back to the short leg's far end, where the short
# leg's own perpendicular partner already is.
#
# Run:  bin/buda flow/mst_shared_leg_suffix.buda --no-viz
# ============================================================

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 33.33
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 33.33

corner_margin dx 10 dy 10

# Same four rectangles as the prefix fixture.  `hub` is still the
# geometric hub — both other targets sit beyond its SW corner —
# but it is now a RECEIVER, so it takes a higher busterm index
# than the driver and its legs arrive as segment ENDS.
add_block drv    600  900   700  950
add_block near  1800  300  1900  400
add_block hub   2000 1000  2100 1100
add_block up    2000 1800  2100 1900

add_bus d[4] drv.tx near.rx,hub.rx,up.rx

run_bundler strict
generate_topologies
run_planner 1
run_nuts

def_track_pattern 4 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4
def_track_pattern 5 0  POWER 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4  GROUND 2 1  (SIGNAL 1 0.5)x4

run_detailed_nuts
dump_topologies --conn
check_design dnuts
report_wirelength
